..................................................................................... 92 Conclusions ................................................................................................................................................. 93 Research Contributions ........................................................................................................................... 93 4 Bradley, T.H., et al., Agent-Based, Bottom-Up Medium- and Heavy-duty Electric Vehicle Economics, Operation, Charging and Adoption, Final Technical Report, DE-EE0009664 Executive Summary This project leverages novel analysis tools jointly developed by Colorado State University (CSU) and the Electric Power Research Institute (EPRI), and up-to-date price and specification data from industry partners including Lightning eMotors (LeM), to simulate uptake, energy use, environmental impacts, and costs of electrified medium-duty and heavy-duty (eMDHD) fleet vehicles. Microsimulations of individual fleets clarify the role and value of charging infrastructure business models and strategies, the costs and benefits expected for fleet composition alternatives, and the effects of transition decisions on throughput, operation, scheduling, and fleet management. Agent-based modeling (ABM) generates bottom-up market projections based on the microsimulation results, interactions between agents, and expected EV adoption programs and initiatives. The aggregated results provide novel insights to the costs and benefits of electrifying various segments of MDHD fleet transport under various policy and market development scenarios. Figure 1. Graphical summary of information flow and tools in DE-EE0009664 5 Bradley, T.H., et al., Agent-Based, Bottom-Up Medium- and Heavy-duty Electric Vehicle Economics, Operation, Charging and Adoption, Final Technical Report, DE-EE0009664 This project was able to achieve the DOE’s goals of developing new modeling tools to understand",
